# Copy to .env and customize. .env is gitignored.
# Read by the orchestrator (tick.py) and sourced by setup.sh. For interactive agent sessions
# (so .mcp.json ${VAR} expansion works), also export these in ~/.zshrc.
#
# IMPORTANT — .env must be shell-clean. setup.sh sources it under bash 3.2, so
# quote any value containing spaces, <, >, or other shell metacharacters, and never
# use a variable name with a hyphen. A malformed line kills every tick.
#
# Every SIDEQUESTOR_* knob below is OPTIONAL and shown with its built-in default. Uncomment
# only what you want to change.

# ── Direct checker connectors ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# External checkers are opt-in by connector. These defaults preserve the established
# Slack, email, GitHub, and Jira behavior. Existing watches for disabled connectors remain
# stored with unchanged watermarks and resume from the same point when re-enabled.
SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_CONNECTORS=slack,email,github,jira
# Add either connector only after installing and authorizing it:
# SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_CONNECTORS=slack,email,github,jira,telegram
# SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_CONNECTORS=slack,email,github,jira,telegram,x

# ── Slack app (create your own — PKCE OAuth, no client secret) ─────────────
# The free Python Slack checkers and reaction sweep are enabled by default and require
# these four values plus the Keychain token installed by setup.sh.
#
# If you have no Slack app and intend to access Slack only from scheduled paid workers
# through Claude/Codex/Cursor MCP, disable the local adapter. Existing slack_* entries
# remain stored with unchanged watermarks; the Python loop quietly ignores them.
# Scheduled and non-Slack watches continue normally.
#
# This legacy Slack-only switch is an additional kill switch. Slack runs only when it is
# present in SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_CONNECTORS and this value is not 0.
# SIDEQUESTOR_SLACK_CHECKERS_ENABLED=0
#
# YAAS talks to Slack via a user-token OAuth app in YOUR workspace, which you
# have to create before these values can exist. Get the ready-to-paste manifest
# with the scopes and redirect URL already filled in:
#
#     sq setup --manifest > slack-app-manifest.yaml
#
# Paste it into https://api.slack.com/apps → Create New App → From an app
# manifest, install it (your workspace admin may need to approve), then copy
# App ID and Client ID from Basic Information into the four values below and
# run setup.sh to authorize it under your own Slack identity.
# The manifest requests Slack Model Context Protocol Server. Before OAuth,
# confirm it is enabled at (manual fallback if workspace policy requires it):
# https://api.slack.com/apps/<SLACK_APP_ID>/app-assistant
#
# These are PUBLIC OAuth identifiers (no client secret — PKCE flow), but they
# are workspace-specific: use your own, don't commit real values back here.
SLACK_APP_ID=
SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
SLACK_WORKSPACE_NAME=
SLACK_WORKSPACE_DOMAIN=

# ── Jira (optional) — jira-call.sh + the dashboard's "open in Jira" links ──
# JIRA_BASE_URL is also what the dashboard uses to build issue/comment links.
# API token: https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens, then
#   security add-generic-password -s jira-api-token -a yaas -w '<TOKEN>'
JIRA_EMAIL=
JIRA_BASE_URL=

# ── Coda MCP — only if you use Coda; leave blank to disable ────────────────
# Get a personal API token at https://coda.io/account → "Generate API token".
CODA_API_KEY=
CODA_MCP_PATH=${HOME}/.local/coda-mcp/dist/index.js

# ── Gmail reply identity — used by $SIDEQUESTOR_RUNTIME_ROOT/yaas-triage/skills/yaas-gmail-reply/ ────
# Format: "Display Name <email@example.com>" — note the quotes; the angle
# brackets are shell redirection operators without them.
SIDEQUESTOR_FROM_EMAIL="Your Name <you@example.com>"

# ── Reaction workflow emojis (optional) ───────────────────────────────────
# Slack emoji names may be written with or without surrounding colons. Every
# value must be unique. The first four trigger actions; loading and done show
# lifecycle progress. Save is one-shot and intentionally skips loading/done.
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_PROCESS_EMOJI=robot_face
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_DRAFT_EMOJI=writing_hand
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_SAVE_EMOJI=floppy_disk
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_ADOPT_EMOJI=incoming_envelope
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_LOADING_EMOJI=hourglass_flowing_sand
# SIDEQUESTOR_REACTION_DONE_EMOJI=white_check_mark

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  SPEND AND DISPATCH CEILINGS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The circuit breaker. On a breach, triage still runs every check (so clean
# watermarks advance and nothing is buried) but withholds the dispatch, writes a
# gate_budget_exceeded event, and fires a desktop notification. Work re-surfaces
# once the window rolls forward or you raise a cap.
#
# These are BREAKERS, not throttles: set them well above your normal rate. Read
# your own numbers first rather than copying these, since cost per dispatch depends
# on how large your worker instruction and quest context files are:
#
#   python3 "$SIDEQUESTOR_RUNTIME_ROOT/yaas-triage/dispatch/spend-window.py" state/run-log.ndjson | jq .
#
# Calibrate these to your own install: watch a few days of normal usage, then set
# the caps above your busiest legitimate hour and well below what an unbounded
# retry loop would spend.

# Hourly dollar cap — the responsive tripwire. Stops a runaway inside the first
# hour instead of letting the daily cap absorb several hundred dollars first.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MAX_SPEND_1H=40

# Daily dollar cap — slow backstop for drift that never trips the hourly cap.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MAX_SPEND_24H=250

# Dispatch-count cap over 6h. This is the FASTEST tripwire (a per-minute runaway
# hits it in about four minutes) and the ONLY one that works when SIDEQUESTOR_AGENT is
# codex or cursor, because those backends report raw tokens with no cost figure.
# Keep it enabled even if you disable the dollar caps.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MAX_DISPATCH_6H=250

# Per-target hourly breaker: stop dispatching one quest that is looping, whatever
# the cause. Set above your busiest legitimate quest (17/hour on the reference
# install) or you will block real work.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MAX_TARGET_DISPATCH_PER_HOUR=25

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  DISPATCH SHAPE
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# One agent invocation per dirty target, run sequentially. Each invocation reloads
# the full rules file, so fan-out multiplies cost per tick — that is the trade for
# a failure in one quest being unable to commit another quest's watermarks.

# Max invocations per tick. Targets beyond this are deferred with their watermarks
# untouched, so the next tick re-detects them.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MAX_DISPATCH_FANOUT=4

# Total dispatch seconds per tick. Each invocation's watchdog is capped to what
# remains, so the triage lock cannot be held far past this.
# SIDEQUESTOR_TICK_DISPATCH_BUDGET=3600

# Don't start a target that cannot get at least this many seconds of runway.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MIN_DISPATCH_SLICE=300

# How many dispatches a watch may go through without the worker closing it in the
# ack ledger before it is promoted to `misconfig` (watermark held, human needed).
# SIDEQUESTOR_UNACKED_PROMOTE=3

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  AGENT BACKEND
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Which CLI runs the worker: claude | codex | cursor. Unset means codex, which is what
# `sq setup` writes unless you pick another backend. Cursor uses the `cursor-agent` CLI.
# SIDEQUESTOR_AGENT=codex
# SIDEQUESTOR_AGENT=claude
# SIDEQUESTOR_AGENT=cursor

# Per-agent worker permissions. Claude accepts its native --permission-mode
# values. Codex supports workspace-write (bounded, network enabled) or
# bypassPermissions (--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox). Cursor uses
# its own CLI execution mode; Sidequestor auto-approves MCP calls for headless work.
SIDEQUESTOR_CLAUDE_PERMISSION_MODE=acceptEdits
SIDEQUESTOR_CODEX_PERMISSION_MODE=workspace-write

# Claude debug logging (troubleshooting). Sets the category filter for `claude --debug`;
# output goes to a per-worker file (logs/worker-<stamp>-<label>.debug) so it shows the
# model/transport layer from the inside — API request/retry/timing and MCP traffic — which
# is what a silent stall hides. Off by default (large output). "api,mcp" is a good filter
# for hang investigations; "all" (or 1/true) logs everything. Leave unset in normal running.
# SIDEQUESTOR_CLAUDE_DEBUG=api,mcp

# Worker watchdogs. Two DIFFERENT axes, both bounding a single worker dispatch:
#
#   SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_TIMEOUT       total wall-clock ceiling — max seconds a worker may run,
#                             no matter how busy. Bounds a runaway-but-active worker
#                             (e.g. a tight loop that keeps emitting output). Default 1800.
#
#   SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_STALL_SECONDS inactivity timeout — kill if NO stream output arrives for
#                             this many seconds. Catches a hung model/transport round-trip
#                             that goes silent (incident 2026-08-08: a worker stalled ~29
#                             min after one tool_use and was killed only at the ceiling).
#                             Can be aggressive because it only trips on silence, so it
#                             won't kill legitimately long active work. Must exceed the
#                             longest legitimate gap between stream lines — a single tool
#                             call's runtime (the Bash tool caps at 600s) — so keep it
#                             above ~600. Default 900. Set 0 to disable (ceiling only).
#
# Keep STALL < TIMEOUT: the stall guard is the fast path for hangs, the ceiling the backstop.
# SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_TIMEOUT=1800
# SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_STALL_SECONDS=900
# SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=15  # dashboard liveness; keep below its 60s stale threshold

# Model overrides, per backend. `sq setup` fills the SELECTED backend's blank model; Claude
# and Codex also get a high reasoning-effort default. Cursor uses its account/CLI default when
# this is unset; set a Cursor model ID here to pin it.
SIDEQUESTOR_CLAUDE_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
SIDEQUESTOR_CLAUDE_EFFORT=high
# SIDEQUESTOR_CODEX_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# SIDEQUESTOR_CODEX_EFFORT=high
# SIDEQUESTOR_CURSOR_MODEL=
# Example: SIDEQUESTOR_CURSOR_MODEL=sonnet-4-thinking

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  TRIAGE LOOP AND CHECKERS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Seconds between ticks, used by triage-loop.sh.
# SIDEQUESTOR_TRIAGE_INTERVAL=60

# Hours a Slack reply may wait before the stale-thread guard routes it for review.
# Fractional values are supported, for example 6.5 for 6 hours 30 minutes.
# SIDEQUESTOR_STALE_REPLY_HOURS=168

# How many quests are checked concurrently. This is the peak number of in-flight
# Slack API calls and is what trips rate-limit detection. Leave low.
# SIDEQUESTOR_TRIAGE_MAX_PARALLEL=3

# Consecutive checker failures before a watch is promoted from exponential backoff
# to `misconfig`. Backoff itself is 60s doubling to a 1h cap and is not tunable.
# SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_ERROR_PROMOTE=6

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  HEALTH MONITOR (the dead-man switch, an instance-specific Sidequestor job)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Runs as its own launchd job so it can detect triage being dead — a check inside
# triage cannot. Install with:
#   sq start
# Check by hand any time with:
#   python3 "$SIDEQUESTOR_RUNTIME_ROOT/yaas-triage/ops/health-monitor.py" --json

# Seconds between health checks.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=300

# Minutes without a COMPLETED tick before "triage is not running" fires. Ticks run
# every 60s, so 10 means ten missed ticks.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_STALL_MIN=10

# Minutes a tick may be in flight before it is called hung. MUST exceed
# SIDEQUESTOR_TICK_DISPATCH_BUDGET (default 3600s = 60min) or long legitimate dispatches
# will be reported as hung.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_HUNG_MIN=75

# Minutes a worker approval lease remains claimable before the dashboard can reclaim it.
# SIDEQUESTOR_APPROVAL_LEASE_MIN=45

# Consecutive non-zero orchestrator (tick.py) exits before alerting. A single non-zero exit is
# normal (a worker failure produces one), so this is deliberately not 1.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_FAIL_STREAK=5

# Minutes an approval may sit in `executing` before it is called stuck.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_APPROVAL_STUCK_MIN=45

# How far back to look for health events in run-log.ndjson, and how long before an
# unchanged condition may notify again.
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_EVENT_LOOKBACK_MIN=60
# SIDEQUESTOR_HEALTH_COOLDOWN_MIN=360

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  RETENTION
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Per-dispatch worker logs (logs/worker-*.{log,ndjson}). 0 = never delete.
# Append-only triage.log/out/err are NOT touched.
SIDEQUESTOR_LOG_RETAIN_DAYS=14

# Default thread retirement age in days. Lower is cheaper: every live slack_thread
# watch is re-polled EVERY tick, and the Slack token has a finite request budget, so
# a long tail of dead threads starves the channel watches that still matter.
# Retires slack_thread watches after this many days without activity.
# Per-quest meta.json can override via "retire_slack_threads_after_days".
# (set to 0/false to disable per-quest).
SIDEQUESTOR_RETIRE_DEFAULT_DAYS=14

# How long a watch marked `ephemeral: true` lives, in hours (168 = one week). The
# dual-watch rule opens a whole-channel watch to catch a top-level reply, which has no natural end: without this
# window it keeps waking its quest on every unrelated message forever, and two quests can
# end up acting on the same one. Only watches the creator MARKED ephemeral expire, so a
# standing subscription (a bot's DMs, a monitored channel) is unaffected by default.
# Per-quest meta.json can override via "retire_ephemeral_after_hours".
SIDEQUESTOR_RETIRE_EPHEMERAL_HOURS=168

# Ack-ledger manifests (state/triage/dispatch-*.json) and per-watch checker health.
# SIDEQUESTOR_MANIFEST_RETAIN_DAYS=7
# SIDEQUESTOR_CHECKER_HEALTH_RETAIN_DAYS=30

# Note: SIDEQUESTOR_WORKER_PERMISSION_MODE is a deprecated alias for
# SIDEQUESTOR_CLAUDE_PERMISSION_MODE and is still read as a fallback. Use the new name.
#
# SIDEQUESTOR_NOTIFY_CMD / SIDEQUESTOR_NOTIFY_REPO_ROOT / SIDEQUESTOR_NOTIFY_WATERMARK / SIDEQUESTOR_TEST_RECORD
# are test-harness overrides only. Do not set them in .env.
